                        "target_lang_word": "mademoiselle",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "mademoiselle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "83089a25-03b5-33cf-8601-635d7b4dea84",
                                        "entry_id": "05663159-0784-3aea-8e2d-2f99fc92f352",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Courtesy title for an unmarried woman in France or a French-speaking country."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9a381bea-3a1f-3a27-9982-c230fd654bc6",
                                        "entry_id": "05663159-0784-3aea-8e2d-2f99fc92f352",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A young woman or girl, especially one who is French or French-speaking."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e8ae097d-422f-4cd5-b374-54821cc31661",
                                                "sense_id": "9a381bea-3a1f-3a27-9982-c230fd654bc6",
                                                "text": "It was a teenage wedding, and the old folks wished them well / You could see that Pierre did truly love the mademoiselle..."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "mademoiselle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                        "entry_id": "ca6be6e3-0467-3699-8dec-3a8120e03397",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To address as “mademoiselle”."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e4462e61-44aa-4c00-8c78-19d53ff234b4",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "If not courted by some tall and handsome “Chasseur,” or some crafty little valet who “mademoiselles” her, she almost invariably condescends to notice favourably some dandified linendraper’s assistant, or sixth clerk in an attorney’s office, whom she met one holiday at the Chaumière or at the Ermitage."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6edeb84b-27b1-478d-a4f4-5d52d5ee1d0a",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "“And if Edla should have a taste and inclination for a military life, would, Mademoiselle wish to made her a general? or, if she had a particular penchant for anatomy, an anatomical professor? Young ladies belong probably to the St. Simonians, and young ladies like these desire that everywhere in civil life the woman should have the same privileges as the man.” (The President always Mademoiselled me very much when he was displeased with me.)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "54ac1d62-1f96-4548-8961-5fb4f4028496",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "A lady, whom the bills—neither Missing, Madaming, nor Mademoiselling—entitled Adelaide Weinthal, appeared as Maffeo Orsini."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d1a7853d-e42b-418d-a95e-5c26063dcb9e",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "There were ladies too, en cheveux, in caps and bonnets, some of whom knew Trilby, and thee’d and thou’d with familiar affection, while others mademoiselle’d her with distant politeness, and were mademoiselle’d and madame’d back again."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "64a543f0-7f29-4f60-8f00-936789e18f28",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "“Mademoiselle, let me mount you on my mare,” I said, “and while you ride on I will get your horse.” She gave a little gasp. “You must not mademoiselle me,” she exclaimed; […] “Mademoiselle,” I said, “it is imperative that we leave the high road. We are, I fear, pursued, and we must throw our pursuers off our track.” “I wish you would not mademoiselle me,” she pouted, dragging on the mare’s mouth."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "387d4fe6-348d-40bf-b47d-7b81542334ea",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "Pamela was not the least bit impressed by the chiaroscuro of attendants mademoiselling her."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "593d0b40-2f07-4d6d-8b1b-5168015bead9",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "First you thought I was a French schoolgirl, and mademoiselled me all over the place, and tonight I look different from the ‘girl’ in chausses you met this afternoon."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8ddd39a7-0904-40a7-8599-7e9a504b795a",
                                                "sense_id": "8ef7bd7a-b100-3868-9bb5-c3bcc66f8f78",
                                                "text": "“I don’t think so, mademoiselle. Irma will not like.” “Don’t mademoiselle me, Pierre, I know all about you guys.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "356192b0-d203-4f74-bb8f-889cefc00e4a",
                        "source_entry_id": "06799e0e-2d47-36c1-a7d6-07e28806181a",
                        "source_sense_id": "7bf7baff-1a7d-3d64-92bc-46c47b6b15eb",
                        "source_sense_description": "französische Anrede für unverheiratete Frauen",
                        "target_lang_word": "mademoiselle",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "mademoiselle",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "27aa6acc-1038-3670-9f6c-7bca9c24cf11",
                                        "entry_id": "e53d5c43-fdc9-35db-a94b-c026d9608064",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "(XIXᵉ siècle) Employé pour désigner, ou s’adresser à, toute femme mariée, roturière ou noble mais non titrée."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b5bfef08-0e96-4d59-b200-1a295f9e1f80",
                                                "sense_id": "27aa6acc-1038-3670-9f6c-7bca9c24cf11",
                                                "text": "Dans cette pièce se tenaient le respectable bourgeois et sa femme, mademoiselle Lecamus."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e3133c30-8977-4753-affc-1fe734f66341",
                                                "sense_id": "27aa6acc-1038-3670-9f6c-7bca9c24cf11",
                                                "text": "On l’appelle mademoiselle, quoiqu’elle ait été mariée. Le nom de dame était encore réservé aux femmes nobles."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b5aa6bfe-9dc0-4a9b-ac95-53518401ac01",
                                                "sense_id": "27aa6acc-1038-3670-9f6c-7bca9c24cf11",
                                                "text": "— Et qui est mademoiselle Marthe ? Est-ce de Marthe que vous voulez parler ? Oui, n’est-ce pas, c’est de celle que vous appeliez Marthe tout court, quand elle était petite ? Eh bien ! pour avoir grandi, elle n’a pas cessé d’être Marthe, et elle n’est pas devenue une demoiselle pour vous, qui avez débarbouillé son nez plein de confitures. Vous lui disiez tu alors, et maintenant vous l’appelez mademoiselle."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "61680705-4b29-40f4-a46d-fd8b82740bf4",
                                                "sense_id": "27aa6acc-1038-3670-9f6c-7bca9c24cf11",
                                                "text": "Elle tenait à sa classe, ne quitta jamais ses coiffes de bourgeoise, ne souffrit jamais d’être appelée que mademoiselle. Les dames nobles l’avaient en haute estime."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3d1195c2-322b-3b3d-b334-ce487e3ffaaf",
                                        "entry_id": "e53d5c43-fdc9-35db-a94b-c026d9608064",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Employé pour désigner, ou s’adresser à, une femme en soulignant son jeune âge ou son statut de célibataire."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "note": "Au Québec, il n’est employé que pour qualifier de très jeunes filles",
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "76c3a073-4f62-4c97-b662-f30bbbc2e8a1",
                                                "sense_id": "3d1195c2-322b-3b3d-b334-ce487e3ffaaf",
                                                "text": "Enfin, en guise de dessert, la cuisinière avait confectionné une sorte de rabote garnie de trois pommes offertes par la bonne du curé, mademoiselle Alice."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f3ab9a56-95cd-45a1-85e7-0a573decec7c",
                                                "sense_id": "3d1195c2-322b-3b3d-b334-ce487e3ffaaf",
                                                "text": "Pardon, mademoiselle, connaissez-vous un bureau de tabac ouvert ?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
